            <ArticleTitle>On the syndrome of the &quot;spare limb&quot;: one case.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>476-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We describe a case of a brain-damaged patient who had a peculiar bodily illusion which could not be labelled an hallucination but seemed somatognosically and phenomenologically similar to the phantom limb without amputation. The patient, who showed left hemiplegia, felt a third upper limb (without seeing it) which he himself defined as &quot;spare.&quot; The spare limb was not deformed; it could be moved and controlled by the patient, and there was no sensation of pain. The patient did not show psychopathological or cognitive disorders. A possible interpretation of the phenomenon is as a &quot;phantom movement&quot; of the paralysed limb: the mental representation of the movement of the limb was dissociated from the bodily representation of his own limb and so was still present in his consciousness despite the paralysis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
